			<a href="https://proton.me/mail/bridge" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">proton.me/mail/bridge</a> and
			<a href="https://proton.me/legal/transparency" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">proton.me/legal/transparency</a>, retrieved 2026-06-21.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>The verdict</h2>
		<div class="verdict-grid">
			<div class="verdict-card verdict-win">
				<h3>Choose Vectis Mail when…</h3>
				<ul>
					<li>You want to <strong>own the infrastructure</strong> — no third party who could be breached, subpoenaed, repriced, or discontinued.</li>
					<li>You want <strong>standard IMAP/SMTP</strong> with any client, no Bridge daemon running on every machine.</li>
					<li>You run <strong>multiple domains or many mailboxes</strong> and want flat, unmetered pricing instead of per-address plan caps.</li>
					<li>You need a <strong>REST sending API, inbound webhooks, or per-domain analytics</strong> — Proton has none of these.</li>
					<li>You want your data in a <strong>standard maildir you can export anytime</strong>, no encrypted-store lock-in.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div class="verdict-card verdict-stay">
				<h3>Stay on (or choose) Proton Mail when…</h3>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>You need the host to be unable to read your mail.</strong> Proton's zero-access encryption is real and Vectis does not replicate it. If that's your hard requirement, Proton wins — full stop.</li>
					<li><strong>You don't want to run a server.</strong> Proton is zero-ops; Vectis means you operate a VPS, manage DNS, and own deliverability.</li>
					<li><strong>You want bundled encrypted calendar, contacts, Drive, and VPN</strong> under one Swiss-law account, with mobile apps that "just work".</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>How they differ</h2>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Two threat models: privacy you rent vs privacy you own</h3>
		<p>
			This is the core of the comparison, and it's worth getting exactly right.
			<strong>Proton Mail's threat model is "the provider must not be able to read my
			mail."</strong> Proton delivers on that: message bodies are protected with
			client-side end-to-end and zero-access encryption, so Proton holds ciphertext it
			cannot decrypt at rest. You are trusting Proton's software and Swiss servers, but
			not trusting them with readable content.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Vectis Mail's threat model is "there should be no provider to trust at
			all."</strong> It's a standard Postfix/Dovecot mail server on your VPS. You — the
			operator — can read mail at rest, because you're the one holding it. There is no
			third party who could be breached, served a warrant, change pricing, or close the
			service. That's the trade: Proton removes the provider's <em>ability</em> to read;
			Vectis removes the <em>provider</em>. Don't read this page as "Vectis is encrypted
			like Proton" — it isn't, deliberately. It's a different answer to a different
			question.
		</p>
		<p>
			One nuance both sides share: a normal email sent to a Gmail or Outlook user is only
			protected by transport TLS once it leaves your control. Neither Proton nor Vectis
			makes that message end-to-end encrypted unless the recipient also uses
			compatible encryption. Proton's E2E is strongest Proton-to-Proton (or via
			password-protected messages); everything else is standard SMTP.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Standard protocols and no lock-in</h3>
		<p>
			Vectis Mail speaks plain IMAP and SMTP. Any client connects directly — Thunderbird,
			Apple Mail, Outlook, mobile, mutt — with no intermediary. Your mail sits in a standard
			maildir on your own disk, so you can back it up, <code>imapsync</code> it elsewhere, or
			walk away whenever you like. There is no proprietary store to be held hostage by.
		</p>
		<p>
			Proton's encrypted architecture means third-party clients can't talk to it natively.
			To use Outlook or Thunderbird you run <strong>Proton Bridge</strong> (paid plans only),
			a local daemon that decrypts and re-exposes your mail over localhost IMAP/SMTP. It works
			well, but it's another moving part on every device, and your mail still lives inside
			Proton's encrypted store. Custom domains are paid-only and capped per plan (one on Mail
			Plus, three on Unlimited). With Vectis, domains and mailboxes are just configuration on a
			server you control.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Cost and ownership</h3>
		<p>
			For one person who wants encrypted mail with no ops, Proton is genuinely cheap:
			Free is $0 (1 GB, one address, no custom domain), Mail Plus is about $3.99/month
			annually, and Proton Unlimited about $9.99/month annually with bundled Drive, VPN, and
			Pass. That's hard to beat for a single encrypted inbox.
		</p>
		<p>
			Vectis Mail's economics flip in your favour the moment you're hosting <em>many</em>
			domains or mailboxes. Starter is $0 for the software (up to 3 domains, 25 mailboxes
			per domain); you pay only for a $5–$20/month VPS. Pro is a flat $39 USD per tenant per
			month — unlimited domains, unlimited mailboxes, the REST API, analytics, and OIDC SSO —
			and one subscription covers every install your organisation runs. Where Proton charges per
			address bracket, Vectis charges per organisation and never per mailbox or per email.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Jurisdiction and what a provider still knows</h3>
		<p>
			Proton's Swiss base is a real privacy asset — Swiss law restricts direct handover to
			foreign authorities. But it's not magic: Proton's 2025 transparency report shows over
			9,000 legal requests with most complied with under Swiss law, and Proton does
			<em>not</em> zero-access-encrypt contact names or primary addresses, so a social graph
			exists that can be compelled. With Vectis there is no provider holding that metadata to
			compel — though, honestly, your VPS host and domain registrar still know who you are, and
			email headers still carry routing data. Self-hosting changes <em>who</em> holds your data
			to "you," which is the entire point.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">What Vectis Mail does NOT do</h3>
		<p>
			Be clear-eyed: Vectis Mail does <strong>not</strong> provide client-side end-to-end or
			zero-access encryption. The operator can read mail at rest. We don't ship an encrypted
			calendar/contacts/Drive/VPN bundle. We don't provide anonymity. And running a mail server
			means you own deliverability — a fresh VPS IP needs warmup, and that's real work Proton
			handles for you. If "the host literally cannot read my mail" or "I never want to touch
			infrastructure" is non-negotiable, Proton is the honest recommendation. Vectis is for
			people whose non-negotiable is the opposite: <em>no host at all</em>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Migrating from Proton Mail to Vectis Mail</h2>
		<p>
			The constraint is Proton's encrypted store: everything must come out through Proton
			Bridge. A realistic sequence:
		</p>
		<ol class="alt-steps">
			<li><strong>Stand up Vectis Mail</strong> on a fresh VPS following the <a href="/getting-started/installation/">installation guide</a>, and configure your domain(s) with DKIM/SPF/DMARC.</li>
			<li><strong>Enable Proton Bridge</strong> (paid Proton plan) to expose your Proton mailbox over local IMAP/SMTP.</li>
			<li><strong>Sync mail</strong> with <code>imapsync</code> from Bridge's localhost endpoint into your Vectis mailboxes — maildir on the Vectis side, standard IMAP throughout.</li>
			<li><strong>Recreate addresses and Sieve filters</strong> on Vectis, and move any custom-domain DNS (MX/DKIM/SPF/DMARC) to point at your server.</li>
			<li><strong>Export calendar and contacts</strong> from Proton separately (iCal/vCard). Vectis doesn't yet import these — CalDAV/CardDAV is on the Phase 4 roadmap.</li>
			<li><strong>Cut DNS over</strong> once mail is flowing through Vectis, then decommission the Proton custom domain after a clean verification window.</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			Note the deliverability reality on the way in: a fresh self-hosted IP needs warmup.
			See <a href="/deliverability/">Will my mail land?</a> and the
			<a href="/guides/ip-warmup/">IP warmup guide</a> before you flip production traffic.
		</p>
	</section>

