				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>What does Vectis Mail cost compared to Proton Mail?</h3>
				<p>
					Proton Free is $0 (one address, 1 GB, no custom domain); Mail Plus is ~$3.99/month
					annually (one custom domain); Proton Unlimited ~$9.99/month annually (three
					domains). Vectis <a href="/pricing/">Starter</a> is $0 for the software — self-hosted,
					up to 3 domains and 25 mailboxes per domain — you pay only for a $5–$20/month VPS.
					Pro is $39 USD per tenant per month for unlimited domains and mailboxes, plus the
					REST API, analytics, and OIDC SSO. Proton is cheaper for one person who wants
					zero-ops encrypted mail; Vectis is flat and unmetered once you host many domains or
					mailboxes.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Is Proton Mail anonymous, and is Vectis Mail?</h3>
				<p>
					Neither is anonymity software. Proton is Swiss-based and encrypts content, but
					operates under Swiss law — in 2025 it received 9,000+ legal requests and complied
					with most, and it can be compelled to log or hand over metadata it holds (contact
					names and primary addresses aren't zero-access-encrypted, so a social graph exists).
					Vectis removes the provider, but your VPS host and domain registrar still know who
					you are, and email headers carry routing metadata. If anonymity is the goal, neither
					tool alone is the answer — both are about email privacy and ownership, not hiding
					identity.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>How do I migrate from Proton Mail to a self-hosted server?</h3>
				<p>
					On a paid Proton plan, run <strong>Proton Bridge</strong> to expose your mailbox over
					local IMAP, then <code>imapsync</code> it into Vectis. Move your custom domain's
					MX/DKIM/SPF/DMARC to point at the Vectis server, recreate addresses and Sieve
					filters, and cut DNS over once mail is flowing. Export contacts and calendar from
					Proton separately (Vectis doesn't yet ship CalDAV/CardDAV — Phase 4 roadmap). The
					Bridge export is the main constraint, since everything must pass through it.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

